# Task family task-89cde052: unresolved guide stripe

Known test geometry: solid full-height vertical color-2 guide near center/right; background 9, with a color-7 irregular compact motif to its left. A demo had background 4, guide in column 14 (0-based), and large color-5 motif at rows 6–15, cols 6–11. Demo output added a full-height color-8 stripe at col 7 (0-based), leaving motif unchanged except for new stripe overlay. Thus color-8 stripe is parallel to guide, at a location associated with motif, not simply an arbitrary fixed distance from guide.

For task test (20x19), guide is col 13, motif spans rows 9–17 and cols 1–4. Eight submitted hypotheses all rejected:
1. full-height 8 at col 0 while preserving input;
2. 8 at col 5;
3. 8 at col 10;
4. 8 at col 0;
5. 8 at col 8 and shift motif one col right;
6. 8 at col 8 with motif shifted right one col;
7. 8 at col 8 plus shifting motif right one col;
8. same col-8 stripe and shifted motif.
The transcript variation reflected confusion over whether to shift pattern; these attempts were rejected and do NOT establish exact error cell or rule.

Key inference from demo: compare before/after exactly. The motif shifts right by one column, and stripe is added at the motif's original left boundary (col 7 in demo), parallel to guide. Test application candidate is therefore shift motif one column toward guide and add stripe on its trailing left edge, but prior col-8 shifted-pattern attempts were rejected. Re-examine coordinate alignment carefully: test motif is 4 cols wide with nontrivial sparse edges, and might require moving toward guide by a larger inferred displacement, or stripe location/shape may derive from the motif's bounding box/centroid. Do not repeat blindly. Best next step: request additional demo if available, explicitly compare translated shape cells, stripe coordinate, and unchanged rows/columns; test candidate transformations cellwise before submission. This task is unresolved. Use continual-arc-recovery protocol; do not assume generic perpendicular-stripe rule.
